      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by noting that \\(\\alpha\\) is inversely proportional to \\(\\beta\\). This means that there exists a constant \\(k\\) such that:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\beta = k\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\alpha = 4\\) when \\(\\beta = 9\\), we can find the constant \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\nk = \\alpha \\beta = 4 \\times 9 = 36\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\). Using the relationship \\(\\alpha \\beta = k\\), we substitute \\(\\beta = -72\\) and \\(k = 36\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\times (-72) = 36\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\alpha\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\frac{36}{-72} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]",
